      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "20a746d0-0f46-53e3-b831-7736b96fc3c6",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: David Bowie, Mick Ronson, Mick Woodmansey, Lulu\n- Songs: \"Memory of a Free Festival\", \"The Man Who Sold the World\"\n- Albums: The Man Who Sold the World, Lulu (1973 album)\n- Topics: studio debuts, release dates, chart performance\n\nFacts:\n- A rock-oriented version of \"Memory of a Free Festival\" marked Mick Ronson's and Mick Woodmansey's studio debut with David Bowie's band.\n- The \"Memory of a Free Festival\" rendition assembled the line-up that would shortly record the album The Man Who Sold the World.\n- David Bowie wrote and performed the song \"The Man Who Sold the World\".\n- \"The Man Who Sold the World\" is the title track of David Bowie's third album The Man Who Sold the World.\n- David Bowie's album The Man Who Sold the World was released in the US in November 1970 and in the UK in April 1971.\n- Lulu's 1973 album failed to chart.\n- A few months after that album's release, Lulu returned to prominence with the hit single \"The Man Who Sold the World\".",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "71aeeeb5-644f-5b9e-b972-2362b33056f5",
        "text": "Page: Memory of a Free Festival. Sentence: A more rock-oriented version than the earlier album cut, this rendition marked guitarist Mick Ronson's and drummer Mick Woodmansey's studio debut with Bowie's band, bringing together the line-up that would shortly record \"The Man Who Sold the World\".\nPage: The Man Who Sold the World. Sentence: \"The Man Who Sold the World\" is a song written and performed by David Bowie.\nPage: The Man Who Sold the World. Sentence: It is the title track of his third album, which was released in the US in November 1970 and in the UK in April 1971.\nPage: Lulu (1973 album). Sentence: The album failed to chart, although Lulu came back to prominence a few months later with the release of the hit single \"The Man Who Sold the World\".",
